> API and interpretation

Complete policy/actions/callback geometry and numeric learning controls are
checked. Actual reset and successor IDs are validated even for terminal steps;
nonfinite rewards/arithmetic fail. Zero episodes invokes no callback, while zero
step cap still resets and records truncation. RLParameters.seed does not seed
environment callbacks, and epsilon is ignored because the supplied policy is
followed. Fresh random-walk factories own independent engines; copies of one
returned environment share its captured engine. Histories retain actual
transitions and initial/post-episode values without replaying callbacks.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Sutton, Learning to Predict by the Methods of Temporal Differences, Machine Learning3 (1988),9-44|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00115009]
- [Sutton and Barto, Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction, second edition, random-walk prediction example|http://incompleteideas.net/book/the-book-2nd.html]

Verified in the shared ten-document batch: 72 scoped native cases;20 directly
instrumented ASan/UBSan cases;82 executable public examples;327/327 definitions
across23 full scopes;10 hovers;3797 independent records/1769203 checks;frozen
valid-input comparisons across all ten families with cyclic-tree validation
correction recorded;12 checkout/installed standalone headers and linked
ten-family consumer;40 executed programs and40 inspected figures with both
exports. Receipts:
`/mnt/projects/datamunge/build/mepml-conversion/batch-384-393/`.
